\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid7343121\charrsid16261365 Explanation}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid7343121 : Extraverts might be more likely to rely on judgment heuristics that are designed to facilitate social harmony (e.g. one
\rquote s responsibility and freedom), regardless of whether everything about a person is said to be caused by previous events. That is, beca
use extraverts are more socially-minded, they may disproportionately encode or perceive features of the individual in the scenario, perhaps drawing on examples from their own social experiences. In these ways, their intuitions may be proportionally less i
n\u-1278\'3fuenced by the deterministic features of the universe and more in\u-1278\'3fuenced by affective or social factors.
\par Individuals high in extraversion (e.g.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid7343121\charrsid13379122  socially}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid7343121 -}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid7343121\charrsid13379122 
minded, outgoing, enthusiastic) are much more likely to judge that a person is free and responsible in a deterministic world than their non-extraverted counterparts}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid7343121 
. These results might partially explain why some philosophical debates are so intractable: People with different personalities, skills, and cognitive representations may simply have different intuitions about philosophical issues, perhaps as a product of 
different (conscious and unconscious) processes.
