world (b) non-reductionist (c) deterministic (d) concrete, and (e) very high affect; (3) a truncated version of the Big-Five Personality Inventory was used that has a very limited 
two prompt extraversion sub-scale; and (4) only non-philosophers were used as participants.
\par The biggest problem is (2)\emdash 
a single scenario. The more concrete and affect-laden a vignette is, the more likely participants will be to have compatibilist intuitions. Given that Feltz and Cokely explicitly state that the intuitions of extraverts may be proportionally less in\u-1278
\'3fuenced by the deterministic features of the universe and more in\u-1278\'3fuenced by affective or social factors, they may have increased the likelihood that participants, regardless of their personality traits, would fail to appreciate the prese
nce of determinism in the vignette. Moreover, they didn\rquote t use any control question}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid3431451 s}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid1595221\charrsid1595221 
 to check whether the participants understood the story.
\par Feltz and Cokely\rquote s results may not reveal anything about compatibilism speci\u-1279\'3f
cally. Rather, extraversion could simply correlate with higher ratings of agreement in general to questions about control and responsibility, or to questions about (or questions that suggest) agents\rquote  deserving blame for their actions\emdash 
the most likely indication of participants\rquote  \'93moral outrage\'94 about an agent\rquote s immoral behavior. To test this interpretation, it would \u-1279\'3frst be helpful to use a control scenario that did not involve deter
minism. The only conclusion we are warranted in drawing at this point is that the moral valence of speci\u-1279\'3fc scenarios can sometimes in\u-1278\'3f
uence the relationship between extraversion and compatibilist intuitions. Perhaps what F&C are tracking is not a direct r
elationship between extraversion and judgments of freedom and responsibility, but rather an indirect relationship between extraversion and a tendency to be susceptible to affective bias.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid1595221 
