\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid16073009\charrsid16073009 moral judgments are often mediated by two classes of brain processes\emdash 
(a) processes that automatically alter hedonic states in response to specific types of socially relevant stimuli, and (b) more domain-general, effortful processes that underlie abilities
 for abstract reasoning, simulation, and cognitive control. Often, these work in unison to foster decisions in accord with the goals of both; goals that are socially adaptive are often congruent with more abstract moral principles. There are dilemmas, how
ever, where those two compete\emdash i.e. dilemmas where one endorses a personal moral violation in order to uphold a utilitarian principle. 
\par I.e. in the footbridge dilemma, which is thus structured, people think it\rquote s wrong to push the guy even though it saves people\rquote s lives. The rare answer \lquote push him\rquote 
 is associated with activation of deliberative centers aimed at cognitive control, suggesting that the automatic negative reaction must be disregarded to choose the utilitarian option.
\par If affective states are a basis for moral judgments for the automatic processes, by manipulating environmental factors separate from any moral violations might in\u-1278\'3fuence affect at the time of judgment and thus the judgment itself.
